@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+---
+kind: upgrade-guide
+description: Portable Electron and global data directories become owned by each bundle directory.
+---
+# Portable data isolation
+
+English | [中文](guide.zh.md)
+
+## Change
+
+Previously only the Harness home lived inside a portable bundle; Electron data, its single-instance lock and some global directories used system paths. Each bundle now owns `home` and `data`, and different directories run concurrently. Inherited `DSH_HOME` and other global directory variables no longer point a portable instance at another installation. Ordinary installations retain their directory rules. Portable mode no longer registers the system `dsh://` protocol automatically.
+
+## Migration
+
+The first automatically created workspace uses `data/documents/deepseek-harness/default-workspace`. Existing workspaces and explicitly selected or configured project directories keep their original locations.
+
+1. Quit the instance normally and back up its portable `home`. Preserve that directory when updating application files: it contains sessions, configuration, credentials and plugins.
+2. First launch creates `data` inside the bundle. Existing system Electron data is neither changed nor copied automatically, so previous shortcut preferences, desktop preferences and browser sign-in state do not appear automatically. To retain preferences, quit the instance and copy its identified `keybindings.json` and `desktop/settings.json` into the corresponding locations under `data/electron`. Do not merge an entire system directory whose instance ownership is unclear.
+3. Copy required shared agent configuration into `home/.agents`. External tools continue to use their original user configuration; no npm or Python configuration migration is required.
+4. Launch bundles from two different directories, create separate sessions and change configuration in each. Restart and verify that sessions, configuration and `data/electron` remain separate. Launching the same directory again should focus the running window.
